 #edb/ref 17592186322060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323613 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de0c8b55-2245-569b-a6f7-434321067ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4c8798a-e8e3-519c-a948-2cb26050a7a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323882 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67c100f5-0b85-5947-a038-3b4c63f6e781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323923 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:  \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345372 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345372 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345372 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02a5c262-cc22-5983-a61f-511098951290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345651 #edb/ref 1080 "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345651 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/