92186348375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348375 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $K$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "def1c542-80f5-5b03-88dc-ad7a755f7e8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348375 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1071 "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "050f4d1f-7ad3-50e3-a5b8-1b02cf764a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348394 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3ab5a48-4ec0-5b84-8cff-a429fb97fc59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379810 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186379816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186379816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379811 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95cac241-a6a2-5133-bd5b-0bc96d341951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf3be306-cf4b-505f-9749-8c4d421cf8bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379813 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "675738a1-186a-5034-b012-faa1a6463030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379814 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2d147f9-5e98-5cd7-a62a-3b200ed85486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40744a34-ee79-5f47-96a8-dad2d3f736ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379816 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7371e87-7e47-503f-b703-0bb0dbb9110d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "912d4ab2-e55a-5e9a-8ddf-b1e59d14eb18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399124 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399125 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a57196c6-f2e4-585a-92ce-6d08ae5e8dd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399126 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399126 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399126 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b30d639-ee9f-5dd6-a783-78b787a8dd54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399127 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399127 